    "zone": "us1.make.com",
    "notes": [
      {
        "moduleIds": [
          1
        ],
        "content": "<p><strong>1 - LEAD FOLLOW-UP</strong></p><p>A new enquiry from your website form is read the moment it arrives and a first reply is written from what they actually asked. Spam and test entries stop at step 3, before anything is sent. If your form tool cannot send to a webhook, step 1 becomes a Make mailhook on the form's notification email and nothing after it changes.</p>",
        "isFilterNote": false,
        "metadata": {
          "color": "#9138FE"
        }
      },
      {
        "moduleIds": [
          5
        ],
        "content": "<p><strong>2 - CRM UPDATES</strong></p><p>The lead is created in GoHighLevel, or updated if they are already there, matched on email and phone so each person is one contact, with UK mobiles saved as +44 so GoHighLevel can text them.</p>",
        "isFilterNote": false,
        "metadata": {
          "color": "#9138FE"
        }
      },
      {
        "moduleIds": [
          6
        ],
        "content": "<p><strong>3 - RELIABLE, CONNECTED, DATA FLOWING CORRECTLY</strong></p><p>Your reliable process, connected systems and data flowing correctly between platforms, grouped here: form, Make and GoHighLevel in one scenario, and a lead GoHighLevel refuses comes to your inbox with the reason. Failed runs nobody sees are the first gap I check in your current setup.</p>",
        "isFilterNote": false,
        "metadata": {
          "color": "#9138FE"
        }
      },
      {
        "moduleIds": [
          7
        ],
        "content": "<p><strong>4 - ONGOING LEAD MANAGEMENT</strong></p><p>What they asked is saved on the contact as a note for whoever calls them back. New leads then get a card in your pipeline, the first text and your follow-up sequence, and someone already in GoHighLevel who enquires again becomes a task for you. Room to add a booking step next, so the card moves when they book.</p>",
        "isFilterNote": false,
        "metadata": {
          "color": "#9138FE"
        }
      }
    ]
  }
}